{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>Upon the two doorposts.</b> Of the entrance of the house, and Moses will further explain how they shall take the blood." "And the word משקוף is from the root of חלון (Genesis 8:6) וישקף (ibid. 26:5), and this משקוף is over the entrance of the house. And thus is the custom in all the land of Ishmael, for the custom of the uncircumcised was not like the custom of the Egyptians, and not even in their foods, as in \"פתות אותה פתים\" (Leviticus 2:6), and thus the adornments of women, to put a ring in the nose and in the ear, and all that Isaiah mentioned concerning the daughters of Zion, and thus they have no house except in a courtyard. The inner one is called פתח הבית, and the outer one שער החצר. And many said that the placing of the blood on the משקוף and on the two doorposts was to show that they would slaughter the abomination of Egypt publicly, for their fear had already fallen upon them, and they would not fear being stoned. But had this been so, it would have been at שער החצר. And behold, the blood was not placed except in secret, for they closed שער החצר, and they slaughtered between the evenings so that no person would see, as it was near night, and no man went out from the entrance of his house until morning, when they went on their way. And each one closed שער חצרו, for the Egyptians thought they would return. Rather, the reason for the blood being on the משקוף is to be an atonement on behalf of all who eat in the house, and to be a sign for the destroyer, that he may see it in the accustomed manner, and \"והתוית תו\" (Ezekiel 9:4)." "And the word בָּתִּים is anomalous in grammar, for there is a dagesh after kamatz gadol, and this is not the rule of the language — unless the kamatz were a chataf, like רָנּוּ שמים (Isaiah 44:23). Perhaps it is so because of the absence of yod in בית, lest this form be confused with the pattern of עשרת הַבַּתִּים חומר (Ezekiel 45:14), which is a plural form, from the ephah and the bat (ibid. 45:11). And the phrase \"על הבתים\" teaches us that it is mil'el, contrary to the custom."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {2 "Resolved the untransliterated Hebrew word משקוף as 'lintel' is deliberately avoided; left in Hebrew since the philological argument about its derivation is central and the term is the subject of discussion."}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:33:34.460450774Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}